<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_check_double — double-checkmark "delivered/read" indicator
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Referenced by:
      - MessageAdapter.java (via binding.ivStatus.setImageResource
        when the peer has read the message, computed from the
        conversation-level lastReadAt comparison)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       All three sub-paths fill with #FFFFFFFF. The actual
       rendered color comes from the ImageView's runtime tint
       set by MessageAdapter:
         - "delivered" (peer received but not read) → muted gray
         - "read" (peer has read) → the "seen" blue accent
       The tint is applied programmatically via
       ImageViewCompat.setImageTintList, not via the layout's
       app:tint, because the tint changes based on the message's
       delivery state.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches ic_check_single.xml exactly. The bubble's
       ImageView declares 14dp × 14dp, so both checkmark
       icons scale down from the same viewport and land at
       the same visual size when rendered. Do NOT change one
       viewport without changing the other — a mismatch
       would produce a jarring size jump when the message
       state transitions.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "DONE_ALL" GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design "done_all" icon
       — the same glyph Google uses in Gmail, Google
       Messages, and every other Material app for
       "delivered/read" in messaging contexts. It is NOT a
       custom double-check composition.

       The icon has three sub-paths that together produce
       the "two checks" visual:
         - Sub-path 1 (small, upper-right): the "tail" of
           the right check, drawn separately for cleaner
           anti-aliasing at the join.
         - Sub-path 2 (large, center): the main body of the
           right check.
         - Sub-path 3 (small, lower-left): the left check
           — the second checkmark that, together with the
           right pair, produces the "double" reading.

       Rendered together, the three sub-paths form the
       familiar double-checkmark. Attempting to "simplify"
       this into a single path would produce either a joined
       shape with a visible seam where the two checks meet,
       or an entirely different shape. Do not modify the
       path data.

    4. PAIRS VISUALLY WITH ic_check_single.
       Both icons share:
         - 2-unit stroke weight
         - Same viewport (24dp)
         - Same right-check geometry (the right check in
           this file IS the same check as ic_check_single,
           just with a second check added to the left)
       The state transition "sent → read" therefore looks
       like a subtle reveal of a second checkmark to the
       left of the existing one, not like an entirely
       different icon appearing. This is the WhatsApp /
       Telegram / iMessage convention and is instantly
       readable by users.

    5. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The double checkmark is a symmetric pair of ticks;
       mirroring under RTL produces no visible change. Do
       not add auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at 14dp inside a bubble:
      Two stacked ticks, crisp at 14dp, immediately readable
      as "delivered" or "read" (depending on tint).
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "done_all" path data.

        Three sub-paths that together produce the double
        checkmark:

          Sub-path 1: "M18,7 l-1.41,-1.41 -6.34,6.34 1.41,1.41 L18,7 z"
            The inner portion of the right check's long
            arm. Drawn as its own closed sub-path for
            anti-aliasing clarity where it meets sub-path 2.

          Sub-path 2: "M22.24,5.59 L11.66,16.17 7.48,12
                       l-1.41,1.41 L11.66,19 l12,-12 -1.42,-1.41 z"
            The main body of the right check — the long
            arm ascending to the upper-right, and the tick
            arm descending to the lower-left.

          Sub-path 3: "M0.41,13.41 L6,19 l1.41,-1.41
                       L1.83,12 0.41,13.41 z"
            The left check — the second checkmark that
            makes this a "double" check. Smaller than the
            right check, positioned in the lower-left of
            the viewport so both checks remain distinct
            and legible at small sizes.

        Do NOT:
          - Merge any two sub-paths into one. The
            join-point anti-aliasing depends on them
            being independent filled shapes.
          - Remove sub-path 1 thinking it "duplicates"
            sub-path 2. Sub-path 1 fills a specific
            geometric sliver that sub-path 2 does not.
            Removing it produces a hairline gap in the
            right check's long arm — visible at 14dp
            on high-DPI screens.
          - Change any coordinate. This is Google's
            official Material Icons asset, byte-for-byte.
            Even a 0.1-unit change produces a visibly
            different glyph.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M18,7 l-1.41,-1.41 -6.34,6.34 1.41,1.41 L18,7 z M22.24,5.59 L11.66,16.17 7.48,12 l-1.41,1.41 L11.66,19 l12,-12 -1.42,-1.41 z M0.41,13.41 L6,19 l1.41,-1.41 L1.83,12 0.41,13.41 z" />

</vector>
